e these CSV files into a TAR file, and stores it in the /log/pm/oss/raw directory. TAR files are available for a rolling default 72-hour period.The OSS may use FTP/SFTP protocols to retrieve TAR files.Below Sequence shows an example for 15 minute performance file generation and transmission.In this example, the performance data generated by the NEs between 21:45 and 22:00 is collected by the EMS some minutes after 22:00. The exact time of collection can be determined by the operator. After collection, the EMS generates the CSV files containing the performance data for the specified time interval. By 22:15, this job finishes, and all the CSV files that have been generated for the original time interval are compressed into one TAR file. The name of this file includes the collection start time ("2145"). The OSS can obtain the file after 22:15./Performance DataPeriodic Performance DataSequence DiagramProcedure for Perodic Performance SFTP over SSHFile Location and File Naming RuleFile NamingVNFCDirectory ReferenceTypeDescription<PSMID>-<YYYYMMDD-HHMM>.tarPSM/log/pm/oss/raw/[product_type]Bundled CSVBundled Performance Data  FileEx) product_type : lte, nr4G: /log/pm/oss/raw/lte/ PSMID-<YYYYMMDD-HHMM>.tar5G: /log/pm/oss/raw/nr/ PSMID-<YYYYMMDD-HHMM>.tar<YYYYMMDD>-<hhmm>_<Measurement Group>_<x.y.z>.csv--CSV? <EMS ID> PSM Identifier? < YYYYMMDD > Collection Date(Year, Month, Day)? < hhmm> Collection Start Time (Hour, Minute)? < Measurement Group> Statistics Group? <x.y.z> SW package name2.5.1.1.2.1 Structure and Contents of CSV FileThe CSV files consist of two sections: header information and content data. The header information includes common fields and counter type names. Values corresponding to each header information field are presented in the content data section of the CSV files. 2.5.1.1.2.2 Header InformationThe header information in the CSV files is divided into three sections: family, version and attribute. Each section starts with the '#' character followed by the section name, a colon ( : ) and the values.Attributes may fall into several categories:Common attributesThis group of attributes is usually used in all PM families.Location attributesThis group of attributes includes index information, and can have different values for each family.Type attributesThis group of attributes includes performance data attributes of a familyTable gives an overview on the structure of header information fields in a CSV file.Overview on the Structure of Header InformationSection NameField NameDescriptionTypeFamily-Name of the performance data familyStringVersion-Software version of the NEStringAttributeNE_IDNE identifier given by the operatorStringSYSTEM_IDNE numeric ID given by the EMSIntegerNE_NAMENE name given by the operatorStringINIT_TIMEDate and time when performance data collection started (format: YYYY-mm-DD hh:24mm:ss)Time StampTIME_OFFSETTimeOffset when performance data collection startedStringGRAN_PERIODGranularity period (15 minutes )IntegerLOCATIONPerformance IndexsStringTYPE#nTypes of a familyString2.5.1.1.2.3 Content DataContent data shows the values corresponding to the attribute field names in the header information. This section presents the values of the NEs managed by the EMS. Values are separated by commas. When a value is absent, then no value and no space is presented between the commas. File content follow#family:Resource#version:20.A.0#index:ShelfId/SlotId#attribute:NE_ID,SYSTEM_ID,NE_NAME,INIT_TIME,TIME_OFFSET,GRAN_PERIOD,LOCATION,MemUsageAvg(%),MemUsageMin(%),MemUsageMax(%),MemUsageTot(%),MemUsageCnt(count),CpuUsageAvg(%),CpuUsageMin(%),CpuUsageMax(%),CpuUsageMaxDuration(s),CpuUsageTot(%),CpuUsageCnt(count),DiskUsageAvg(%),DiskUsageMin(%),DiskUsageMax(%),DiskUsageTot(%),DiskUsageCnt(count)eNB_11043,11043,ENB_11043,2020-01-30 08: